OmniSigil::checkAction
├── given the config was never initialized
│   └── it should revert with PolicyNotInitialized
├── given a static-offset recipient rule (v2-style router swap)
│   ├── when the recipient word equals the locked account
│   │   └── it should return VALIDATION_SUCCESS
│   └── when the recipient word is an attacker
│       └── it should return VALIDATION_FAILED
├── given a recipient nested in dynamic calldata (UniversalRouter execute)
│   ├── when the same call is re-encoded with a different commands length
│   │   └── it should move the recipient to a different offset
│   └── when a static rule tuned for one encoding meets another valid encoding
│       ├── it should still reject the attacker under the tuned encoding
│       └── it should wrongly reject the legitimate call under the other encoding
├── given a single EQUAL rule
│   ├── when the param equals the ref
│   │   └── it should return VALIDATION_SUCCESS
│   └── when the param differs from the ref
│       └── it should return VALIDATION_FAILED
├── given a single GREATER_THAN rule
│   ├── when the param is strictly greater than the ref
│   │   └── it should return VALIDATION_SUCCESS
│   └── when the param equals the ref
│       └── it should return VALIDATION_FAILED
├── given a single LESS_THAN rule
│   ├── when the param is strictly less than the ref
│   │   └── it should return VALIDATION_SUCCESS
│   └── when the param equals the ref
│       └── it should return VALIDATION_FAILED
├── given a single GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL rule
│   ├── when the param equals the ref
│   │   └── it should return VALIDATION_SUCCESS
│   └── when the param is strictly less than the ref
│       └── it should return VALIDATION_FAILED
├── given a single LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL rule
│   ├── when the param equals the ref
│   │   └── it should return VALIDATION_SUCCESS
│   └── when the param is strictly greater than the ref
│       └── it should return VALIDATION_FAILED
├── given a single NOT_EQUAL rule
│   ├── when the param differs from the ref
│   │   └── it should return VALIDATION_SUCCESS
│   └── when the param equals the ref
│       └── it should return VALIDATION_FAILED
├── given a single IN_RANGE rule (ref packs min<<128 | max)
│   ├── when the param sits inside the inclusive range
│   │   └── it should return VALIDATION_SUCCESS
│   ├── when the param is below the minimum
│   │   └── it should return VALIDATION_FAILED
│   └── when the param is above the maximum
│       └── it should return VALIDATION_FAILED
├── given an AND tree over two rules
│   ├── when both rules pass
│   │   └── it should return VALIDATION_SUCCESS
│   └── when only one rule passes
│       └── it should return VALIDATION_FAILED
├── given an OR tree over two rules
│   ├── when neither rule passes
│   │   └── it should return VALIDATION_FAILED
│   └── when exactly one rule passes
│       └── it should return VALIDATION_SUCCESS
├── given a NOT tree over one rule
│   ├── when the inner rule fails
│   │   └── it should return VALIDATION_SUCCESS
│   └── when the inner rule passes
│       └── it should return VALIDATION_FAILED
├── given a nested tree ((A AND B) OR (NOT C))
│   ├── when the left AND branch holds
│   │   └── it should return VALIDATION_SUCCESS
│   ├── when only the right NOT branch holds
│   │   └── it should return VALIDATION_SUCCESS
│   └── when neither branch holds
│       └── it should return VALIDATION_FAILED
├── given a valueLimitPerUse cap
│   ├── when the action value is at the cap
│   │   └── it should return VALIDATION_SUCCESS
│   └── when the action value exceeds the cap
│       └── it should revert with ValueLimitExceeded
└── given a limited rule that accrues usage
    ├── when a single use is within the cumulative limit
    │   └── it should return VALIDATION_SUCCESS
    ├── when a single use exceeds the cumulative limit
    │   └── it should return VALIDATION_FAILED
    └── when two uses together cross the cumulative limit
        ├── it should pass the first use
        └── it should fail the second use that overflows the accrued total
